cessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.portability"],"criteria":["project.failure-disposition-contract"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.431,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'A dirty or diverged workspace is never mutated — it fails loud and is left exactly as found'","'An unreachable remote leaves the workspace untouched'","ABSENCE: no disposition word (retryable/transient vs fatal/permanent) per arm; no statement of what the gate/consumer sees after a refusal — does provisioning abort, or does the stale-tree gate proceed?"],"finding":"The plan adds refusal arms (dirty, diverged, unreachable remote) but does not state the DISPOSITION the provisioning caller surfaces per arm — specifically whether a refusal aborts container startup / fails the gate loudly, or whether the gate then runs against the stale tree anyway. The unreachable-remote arm ('leaves the workspace untouched') reads as transient/retryable while dirty/diverged is a persistent condition requiring break-glass, but the plan never labels which are retryable vs fatal, nor what happens downstream when the refresh refuses.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f766b21e6c391947d","impact":0.536,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — 'it fails loud and is left exactly as found'; AC 'A refusal names its condition'","norm_id":"n6fd7466cbfbd1189","priority":0.469,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Unreachable remote on a transient network blip: does provisioning retry-then-proceed on the last-good tree, or hard-fail the container? The plan leaves this to implementation.","Dirty/diverged refusal: does the gate still run against the now-known-stale tree (re-introducing the original silent-drift bug) or does the refusal block the gate loudly?"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.875,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"na","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"medium"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State whether gate consumers acquire the same checkout lock while reading MCP_CODE_DIR, so the fast-forward cannot mutate the tree mid-read (close the TOCTOU the plan raises for reset but not for ff-merge).","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","T9","necessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.portability"],"criteria":["T9"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.851,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'take the existing checkout lock, require a clean tree, git fetch origin main, then git merge --ff-only origin/main'","'Silently resetting a workspace a gate may be mid-read on would trade one correctness bug for another' — acknowledges the mid-read hazard but only for reset, not for ff-merge","ABSENCE: the plan does not state that gate consumers acquire the same checkout lock while reading, so lock coverage of the read side is unverified from the plan text"],"finding":"The plan takes 'the existing checkout lock' for the fast-forward but never states whether the gate consumers that READ the workspace acquire that same lock. If gates read the tree without holding the checkout lock, a concurrent ff-merge is a read-during-mutation (TOCTOU) that the plan's 'a gate may be mid-read' reasoning acknowledges but does not close.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f8a898a0e0a7bc43d","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — 'take the existing checkout lock, require a clean tree'","norm_id":"nda52f034a25d972e","priority":0.099,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A completion/plan-review gate reads MCP_CODE_DIR mid-fast-forward and observes a tree in transition, producing a verdict against a half-updated tree"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.375,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"low","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Establish that the ff-merge is serialized against concurrent gate reads/ref-resolution (name the lock the gates actually take — the git-common-dir fetch lock, not the entrypoint's directory-inode lock) or justify why an in-place `git merge --ff-only` on the live workspace cannot produce a torn read for a gate mid-resolution.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.4827,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["mcp-entrypoint.sh:69-83 `with_dir_lock` locks the directory inode via `exec 9<\"$wdl_dir\"; flock ... 9` — a per-inode advisory lock taken only by provisioning writers","src/rebar/_store/git_locking.py:311-350 gate ref-updating fetches take `fetch_coordination_lock` keyed on the git COMMON dir (`rebar-fetch.lock`), and gate ref resolution runs `fetch=False` (per parked ticket a2e5-094d reading of gate_source.py:89-109) — neither takes the entrypoint's directory-inode lock","Plan's own rationale rejects resetting a tree 'a gate may be mid-read on', yet `git merge --ff-only origin/main` also mutates a live tree's HEAD/refs/worktree that a concurrent gate reads"],"finding":"The plan asserts the refresh 'take[s] the existing checkout lock', implying the ff-merge is safe against a gate mid-read, but the entrypoint's checkout lock and the gate's read/fetch lock are different lock identities, so the permitted fast-forward can mutate HEAD/refs/worktree under a concurrent gate.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f4cac280d5f740a65","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — 'take the existing checkout lock' / 'Silently resetting a workspace a gate may be mid-read on'","norm_id":"nba60fd0bf87c1ff3","priority":0.1173,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A gate resolves origin/main or a pinned SHA in MCP_CODE_DIR at the same instant a boot/restart-triggered provision runs the ff-merge, observing a moved/torn HEAD or partially-updated worktree"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.4444,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"no","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"low","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State how boot-time-only refresh bounds workspace drift for a long-lived `--restart always` server (e.g. is a redeploy/restart guaranteed frequent enough), or extend the trigger so drift cannot grow unbounded between restarts.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":"novelty","evidence":["mcp-entrypoint.sh:237 `provision_store &` runs provisioning once at container start, then `exec \"$@\"` boots the long-lived server","autodeploy.sh:382 `--restart always` — the container is expected to stay up between deploys","Problem: 'The drift is unbounded and silent' and the incident 'was 130 commits behind origin/main' occurred on a running container, not at a boot boundary"],"finding":"The refresh is triggered only on the provisioning path, which runs once per container start (`provision_store &`), but the MCP server is long-lived (`--restart always`); so a rarely-restarted server's workspace still drifts unboundedly between restarts — the very failure mode the Problem section names (the incident was 130 commits behind on a running container).","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f4efcdcea09073b39","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — 'Refresh the workspace on the path that already owns its lifecycle' / Problem","norm_id":"nc15a778162460423","priority":0.0,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A container runs for weeks without restart; mai